PITFALLS = {
    "graph_definition": "Not everything is a graph — a sorted shopping list has no relationships, so 'graph' would add nothing.",
    "vertices_edges": "An edge joins exactly TWO vertices. 'A, B and C are all friends' is three edges, not one.",
    "directed_vs_undirected": "Real systems mix both: one app can have mutual friendships AND one-way follows — model each relation with its own edge type.",
    "weighted_vs_unweighted": "'Unweighted' does not mean edges are free — every edge still costs exactly one step. That single step is what BFS counts.",
    "degree": "In directed graphs, degree splits into in-degree and out-degree — 'the degree' is ambiguous until you say which.",
    "path": "A path must follow edges that actually exist. 'They look close on the drawing' means nothing without an edge.",
    "cycle": "Going A → B → A uses the SAME edge twice — that is not a cycle. A cycle needs distinct edges and must return to its start.",
    "connectedness": "Connected does not mean complete: vertices don't need direct edges to each other — a path through other vertices is enough.",
    "edge_list": "Neighbor lookup in an edge list is O(E) per query — use it for storage/input, not for traversal-heavy algorithms.",
    "adjacency_matrix": "Memory explodes quadratically: 1M vertices need 10¹² cells. Also, undirected graphs waste a symmetric half of the matrix.",
    "adjacency_list": "Asking 'is B a neighbor of A?' costs O(degree(A)), not O(1) — if your algorithm needs millions of those checks, think twice.",
    "choosing_representation": "There is no universally best representation — the right choice depends on the dominant operation (lookup vs iteration vs memory).",
    "why_traversal": "Plain traversal finds shortest paths ONLY in unweighted graphs. Weighted 'cheapest route' questions need different algorithms (beyond V0).",
    "traversal_state": "Forgetting to seed the state (visited = {start}, frontier = [start]) is the classic bug — an empty frontier means nothing ever runs.",
    "visited_state": "Marking too late is the trap: mark on enqueue (BFS) or on entry (DFS), never at dequeue time, or vertices pile up in the structure.",
    "traversal_order": "Visit order follows the neighbor listing order in your adjacency structure — it is not alphabetical and not 'canonical'.",
    "manual_tracing": "The trace must be boring: one vertex per step, frontier and visited updated exactly as the code does. Skipping steps hides the bug you're hunting.",
    "bfs_intuition": "BFS gives shortest paths by EDGE COUNT in unweighted graphs — say 'unweighted' out loud; with weights, BFS's first reach is no longer optimal.",
    "queue_mechanism": "popleft() and pop() differ by one word but produce different algorithms: BFS vs DFS-like order. Pick the end deliberately.",
    "bfs_visited_handling": "Mark-on-dequeue lets the same vertex enter the queue many times — on dense graphs this quietly blows up time and memory.",
    "bfs_manual_tracing": "Record the queue contents BEFORE and AFTER each dequeue — that is where level-order structure (and your mistakes) become visible.",
    "bfs_implementation": "Two classic bugs: forgetting visited = {start} before the loop, and marking neighbors after enqueueing instead of before.",
    "bfs_time_complexity": "O(V) alone ignores edge scanning; O(V²) applies to adjacency matrices, not lists. State the representation before the bound.",
    "bfs_space_complexity": "Don't forget visited itself is O(V) — the queue is only the second half of the memory story.",
    "dfs_intuition": "DFS is not a shortest-path algorithm — deep-first arrival order has no distance guarantee. Don't reach for it when 'fewest steps' is asked.",
    "recursion_mechanism": "No base case (or no guaranteed progress) means RecursionError — and Python caps recursion depth around 1000, which deep paths can hit.",
    "dfs_visited_handling": "Marking after exploring neighbors allows the same vertex to be pushed twice — on a cycle that is infinite recursion.",
    "dfs_manual_tracing": "Backtracking is part of the trace: when a call returns, show the stack unwinding — depth structure is the whole point of DFS.",
    "dfs_implementation": "Two traps: creating a fresh visited set inside each call (memory resets, infinite recursion), and mutable default arguments (visited=set() in the signature).",
    "dfs_time_complexity": "The call stack is not 'extra work' — time stays O(V+E). Don't add a log factor or a second V term just because recursion is involved.",
    "dfs_space_complexity": "It is the CALL STACK that reaches O(V), not a queue. Deep graphs can overflow it — which is exactly why explicit-stack DFS exists.",
    "explicit_stack_dfs": "Without reversed() the pop order (and thus visit order) differs from recursive DFS — and stale stack duplicates are fine only because of the visited-skip.",
    "bfs_vs_dfs": "Both are O(V+E) — 'which is faster' is the wrong question. The right question: does the problem want levels (BFS) or depth (DFS)?",
    "recognizing_traversal": "Weighted 'cheapest/shortest cost' problems are NOT plain traversal — recognizing that boundary is itself a skill.",
    "traversal_application": "Most application bugs are modeling bugs: wrong vertices, wrong edges, or missed direction. Get the graph right before any code.",
    "v0_readiness": "Memorized code without the 'why' fails the readiness check — you must produce, trace, and explain independently, not recite.",
    "adjacency": "'Connected to' by a PATH is not adjacency — A→B→C makes A and C reachable, but only A-B makes them adjacent.",
    "conversion_between_representations": "Converting is not free at scale: list→matrix trades O(V+E) memory for O(V²) — convert only when the target representation earns its cost.",
    # Layer 1 pitfalls
    "traversal_boundary": "The traversal stopping does not mean it failed — it means the component is done. Unvisited vertices are in other components, not a bug.",
    "component_discovery": "An unvisited vertex after traversal does not mean 'BFS missed it'. It means 'this vertex is in another component — start a new traversal here'.",
    "component_enumeration": "The outer loop runs for every vertex, but the inner BFS runs only for unvisited ones. Thinking it runs V times BFS = O(V(V+E)) is the classic mistake. The visited set guarantees each vertex is processed once total.",
    # Layer 2 pitfalls
    "bfs_distance": "Shortest means fewest EDGES, not fewest vertices — and the guarantee holds only for unweighted graphs. Also, visited and distance are NOT the same information: -1 just happens to double as 'not discovered' in our distance-array convention.",
    "path_reconstruction": "Parents are set at FIRST discovery and never overwritten — chasing overwrites is impossible, but expecting BFS to return ALL shortest paths is the classic trap: it returns one valid path, chosen by adjacency order. And don't forget to reverse: the backward walk produces the path in reverse.",
    # Layer 3 pitfalls
    "cycle_encounter_reasoning": "A visited neighbor is NOT automatically a cycle: on an undirected graph even a tree shows a visited neighbor at every vertex — the parent. Read the observation only together with 'is this the vertex I arrived from?'",
    "parent_aware_traversal": "The parent check is per CALL — the parent of the current vertex only. A vertex visited by an earlier branch is not your parent even though it is adjacent, and that encounter is exactly the evidence you must not skip. Skipping is also surgical: the parent edge once per endpoint, every other edge never.",
    "undirected_cycle_detection": "Three classic traps: (1) one traversal from vertex 0 says nothing about other components — use the outer loop; (2) in the BFS variant, marking at dequeue instead of enqueue can put a vertex in the queue twice and fake cycle evidence; (3) the parent must travel per recursive call (per queue entry) — one global 'previous vertex' collapses as soon as a vertex branches.",
    # Layer 4 pitfalls
    "two_color_constraint": "Bipartite does NOT mean 'exactly two vertices' or 'exactly two components' — it means the vertices split into two groups with every edge crossing between them. And not every graph admits a split: whether one exists is the whole question.",
    "color_propagation": "Colors alternate per BFS LEVEL only because every discovery comes from the previous level — the rule is 1 - color[current], not 'even levels only'. Also remember the color array replaced the visited set: coloring a vertex twice is the bug to avoid.",
    "color_conflict_detection": "'Already colored' is not 'conflict': every non-tree edge meets a colored neighbor, and opposite colors are perfectly fine. Only color[nbr] == color[v] violates the invariant. And one conflict anywhere — in ANY component — decides the whole graph.",
    "bipartite_graph": "Every cycle is NOT a problem — only ODD cycles are (walk a square: 0,1,0,1 closes cleanly; walk a triangle: 0,1,0 clashes). BFS and DFS verdicts must always agree because bipartiteness belongs to the graph, not the exploration order. A self-loop is an odd cycle of length one: not bipartite.",
}
